   <section class='publication-abstract'><h2 class='publication-abstract-heading'>Abstract</h2><div class='publication-abstract-body'>VICReg organizes noncontrastive representation learning around invariance,
marginal variance, and cross-coordinate covariance. Its usual batch statistics and
two-view computation do not directly yield a causal, neuron-local update.
Temporal Regularized Learning (TeReL) converts this decomposition into a
causal surrogate: adjacent observations form the positive pair, detached
running moments replace batch statistics, and a lagged same-layer operator
supplies a covariance signal. Temporal adjacency turns invariance into
slowness, making the construction a soft form of Slow Feature Analysis.
Detaching historical quantities truncates cross-time credit assignment
without removing the current-observation gradient. At each layer, a
detached regularized target maps its residual to the state of each neuron.
The feedforward update then factors into a presynaptic value and this
postsynaptic state; one lateral matrix corrects the state once and receives an
anti-Hebbian state–state contribution. The resulting encoder processes one
observation at a time, sends no error signal across learned layers or time,
and stores three causal scalars per neuron together with one matrix of
auxiliary lateral parameters per layer. On a class-chunked MNIST stream,
batch-size-one stochastic-gradient training reaches <span class='mathjax-inline'>\\(96.39\\pm 0.19\\%\\)</span> held-out linear-probe
accuracy.</div></section>
